      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the mid‑1800s, Stringtown—later Boles—thrived on agriculture, blacksmiths, grist mills, and a cotton gin, growing to 100 residents by 1887.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/boles_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Archaeological evidence indicates long‑term human occupation in the Boles area, with mound sites linked to Caddo traditions dating back to the Mississippian period.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/boles_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The 1820 Treaty of Doak’s Stand opened the region for U.S. settlement, but subsequent treaties in 1825 displaced about 200 families, paving the way for white settlers.",
